    "translation": "Supreme felicity (Release/Moksha) is attained by the knowledge of the true nature of sixteen categories: Means of Right Knowledge (Pramana), Objects of Right Knowledge (Prameya), Doubt (Samsaya), Purpose (Prayojana), Familiar Instance (Dristanta), Established Tenet (Siddhanta), Members of a Syllogism (Avayava), Confutation (Tarka), Ascertainment (Nirnaya), Discussion (Vada), Wrangling (Jalpa), Cavil (Vitanda), Fallacy (Hetvabhasa), Quibble (Chhala), Futility (Jati), and Point of Defeat (Nigrahasthana).",
    "commentary": "The foundational aphorism of the Nyaya-Sastra, formulating its entire epistemological and dialectical architecture in a single copulative compound (Itaretara-Dvamdva). Vatsyayana and Uddyotakara emphasize that human civilization rests upon four sovereign sciences (Catasro Vidyah): Trayi (the Vedic triple science of ritual and heaven), Varta (agriculture, trade, and economic sustenance), Dandaniti (politics, statecraft, and public order), and Anviksiki (the science of critical inquiry and logic). If Gautama had mentioned only Pramana (instruments of knowledge) and Prameya (objects of knowledge like the soul), Nyaya would have collapsed into Upanishadic spiritual science (Adhyatma-vidya), reducing the four sciences to three. By explicitly enunciating Doubt, Motive, the Five Syllogistic Members, and the Three Modes of Debate (Vada, Jalpa, Vitanda), Nyaya establishes its distinct, irreplaceable domain (adhikara).\n\nVatsyayana defines Nyaya as 'Pramanair artha-pariksanam nyayah' — the critical examination of things by means of the valid instruments of knowledge. It is called Anviksiki because of 'anu-iksa' (reviewing or scrutinizing through inference what has already been apprehended by perception and verbal testimony). An inference that contradicts direct perception is not genuine reasoning, but pseudo-logic (nyayabhasa), because perception is immediately sublating (badhita). The five syllogistic members (avayava) represent the supreme form of reasoning (paramo nyayah) because they synthesize all four pramanas into a single unified proposition: Pratijna embodies Sabda, Hetu embodies Anumana, Udaharana embodies Pratyaksa, Upanaya embodies Upamana, and Nigamana synthesizes them all.\n\nVatsyayana concludes his exposition with the immortal verse: 'Pradipah sarvavidyanam upayah sarvakarmanam / Asrayah sarvadharmanam sasvad anviksiki mata //' (Nyaya is eternally recognized as the lamp of all sciences, the resource of all practical actions, and the foundation of all righteous duties).",
