    "translation": "Pain, birth, activity, defect, and misapprehension; on the successive annihilation of these in the reverse order, there follows Release (Apavarga).",
    "commentary": "Formulates the exact, irreversible reverse causal sequence (uttarottarāpāye tadanantarāpāyāt) governing bondage and salvation, grounded in Vātsyāyana's Bhāṣya and Uddyotakara's Vārtika.\n\n1. The Problem of Immediate Release vs. Pedagogical Lineage: In the Introductory Vārtika, Uddyotakara raises a vital dilemma: Does Release follow immediately upon the flash of True Knowledge? If release (as cessation of embodied existence) occurred instantaneously upon realizing knowledge, the knower would immediately die. But then no master could survive to teach disciples, destroying the unbroken lineage of scientific tradition (śāstra-sampradāya-paramparā)! If, to avoid this, the enlightened person continues to live, how can knowledge be called the cause of release? Uddyotakara resolves this by distinguishing two levels of emancipation: Lower Release (Apara-Apavarga) and Higher Release (Para-Apavarga). Lower Release arrives immediately upon True Knowledge: defects vanish, no new karma is created, and the sage lives free from emotional bondage to teach the science. Higher Release (absolute Videhamukti) occurs gradually when residual prārabdha karmic momentum is exhausted and the body drops off.\n\n2. The Nature of Pravṛtti: Why does the sūtra state that Activity (Pravṛtti) causes Birth, when physical and vocal actions are momentary (kṣaṇika)? Vātsyāyana explains that 'Pravṛtti' here metonymically denotes its enduring karmic fruits: Merit (Dharma) and Demerit (Adharma). Just as we say 'food is life' because food sustains life, 'activity' denotes the unseen karmic impressions (adṛṣṭa) that compel reincarnation.\n\n3. Why Renounce Pleasure Along with Pain (Madhu-viṣa-saṃpṛktānna-nyāya): Why must the seeker abandon pleasure if only pain is undesirable? Vātsyāyana and Uddyotakara prove that worldly pleasure is inextricably bound up with pain: (a) Invariable concomitance: no worldly pleasure is free from fear of loss or alloyed distress; (b) Identical causes: senses and bodies that experience pleasure are the very instruments of affliction; (c) Shared substratum: pleasure and pain reside in the exact same physical body; and (d) Identical conscious agency: the mind feels both. One cannot selectively separate them; seeking pleasure drags in pain, just as eating food mixed with honey and poison (madhu-viṣa-saṃpṛktānna) brings death.\n\n4. Eradication of False Notion: True Knowledge contradicts Wrong Notion because one object cannot possess contradictory natures. Error is unsupported (nirālambana), whereas True Knowledge is supported by the actual object (sālambana) and backed by the three-stage process of cognitive realization: Scriptural Testimony (Śabda) -> Rational Scrutiny (Manana/Anumāna) -> Concentrated Meditation (Bhāvanā), culminating in vivid Direct Perception (Pratyakṣa) that banishes ignorance forever.",
