    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): The sutra defines Upamana as prasiddha-sadharmyat sadhya-sadhanam — accomplishing the unknown through similarity to the well-known. The stock case: a villager who knows the cow hears from a forester 'as the cow so the gavaya'; going to the forest and perceiving a cow-like animal, he cognizes 'this is called gavaya'. The fruit (phala) is knowledge of the name–name-bearer relation (samjna-samjni-sambandha), while the instrument (pramana) is the similarity-cognition itself. Upamana is thus the means of denotative (naming) knowledge, irreducible to seeing or hearing alone.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Three moments in order — (1) verbal cognition of the co-reference sentence, (2) forest perception of the strange animal (perception-factor), (3) remembrance of the sentence joined with perceived cow-likeness, culminating in denotative cognition. Against the Buddhist reduction to inference: no invariable concomitance (vyapti) of the form 'whatever is cow-like is a gavaya' operates as probans with subject-presence (paksadharmata); against reduction to testimony: bare words without presently perceived similarity never yield 'this is gavaya'. Uddyotakara cites this very sutra as the model of collective definition (samudaya-paksa) — the whole phrase forms one definition, unlike 1.1.10 where each mark defines severally.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Prasiddha' means well-known to this observer (the cow), 'sadhya' the not-yet-denoted (gavaya-hood and its name). The charge of proving the proved (siddha-sadhana) fails because what is accomplished is new: a denotative link never before cognized. He fixes the pramana–phala split: similarity-cognition is instrument, naming-cognition is fruit.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Against Prabhakara Mimamsa, which compresses analogy into extended application (atidesa) of a remembered sentence, Udayana insists the presently perceived similarity is indispensable — a blind man with the sentence alone never learns 'gavaya'. Similarity must be cognized as qualifying this animal now, not merely remembered.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Notes the same 'as that, so this' factor reappears inside inference itself — the minor-premise application ('as the hearth so this hill') is analogical in structure, yet inference and analogy differ in fruit (fire-cognition vs naming-cognition), preserving the fourfold restriction.",
