    "translation": "Verbal Testimony (Word) is the instructive assertion of a reliable person (Apta).",
    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Sabda is aptopadesa — the teaching-assertion of one who has attained truth. Apta is defined by three marks: direct knowledge of the thing (saksatkara), freedom from deceit, and compassionate desire to communicate as known. Crucially the apta may be rsi, arya, or even mleccha (outsider): reliability is virtue-based, not birth-based — a foreign physician on herbs is apta for herbs. This founds both worldly expertise and Vedic authority in one stroke, since the Veda is the teaching of the supreme aptas.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Records the compound dispute — 'aptopadesa' as 'the assertion OF a reliable person' versus 'apta-upadesa' as 'a reliable assertion'. The second reading is proposed so that unseen things (heaven, apurva, deity), imperceptible to us, can still be denoted; for if no one perceived them, assertion about them would be baseless. The reply: sages perceive the suprasensible by extraordinary contact, so the speaker-side reading stands — reliability resides in the person, since identical words from a deceiver generate no knowledge. Against the Mimamsaka, who makes the Veda authorless to secure infallibility, Uddyotakara holds apta-authorship (culminating in Isvara as parama-apta) is compatible with unbetraying truth; against the Carvaka, who denies all unseen testimony, he shows daily life already trusts the unseen (a farmer trusting rain-lore).\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): Dissects apta-laksana into (i) yathartha-darsitva (seeing truly), (ii) akaitava (non-deceit), (iii) parisphuta-vivaksa (clear desire to teach). He defends the mleccha clause: were aptatva birth-restricted, travellers could never learn frontiers from frontiersmen. 'Upadesa' is fenced as instructive communication (vidhi-pratisedha-paraka is wider; upadesa teaches the unknown).\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Words denote positively through conventional denotative power (sanketa-sakti), not merely by excluding others as the Buddhist apoha-vada claims; the apta guarantees the connection is non-belying (avisamvadi). Sentence-meaning needs expectancy, fitness, and proximity (akanksa-yogyata-sannidhi) — the apta's sentence satisfies all three, so hearer-cognition is prama, not guess.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Bridges to 1.1.8 — the very need for drsta/adrsta division is latent here, since apta ranges over both perceived and suprasensible knowers. PANINI: apta = ap (to attain) + kta (one who has attained truth); upadesa = upa-dis + ghan (instructive address); sabda = 'sabdyate anena' (that by which objects are denoted).",
