    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): 'Sa' (testimony) is twofold by object. Drstarttha bears on the perceptible — the physician's regimen to the patient, the witness's report of the road — checkable here by perception. Adrstarttha bears on the imperceptible — the Vedic injunction 'he who desires heaven should sacrifice with the Jyotistoma', dharma/adharma, apurva, soul, liberation — whose fruit no eye sees. Both are pramana because both stem from aptas; checkability is accidental to the object, not constitutive of the instrument.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): The objector presses — heaven, apurva, deity are perceived by none of us, so no assertoric activity (sabda-pravrtti) can bear upon them; better read the compound as 'apta-upadesa', a reliable assertion, dropping the speaker. Reply: 'not perceived by us' is not 'perceptible by none' — sages and yogins perceive the suprasensible by extraordinary contact, and transmit; what we mean is never that heaven is seen by the hearer. The speaker-reading therefore stands, and with it the twofold division: testimony tracks the apta's range, which spans both domains.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): Sharpens drsta as perceptible-in-principle (including the inferable, like fire from smoke when taught) versus adrsta as what ordinary perception can never reach (heaven's bliss, apurva, post-mortem states). He secures Vedic vidhi (injunction) as pramana with unseen fruit: its authority needs no perceived result, only an unbetraying source — just as a son trusts the father's 'poison kills' without tasting. Arthavada (eulogy) and anuvada rest on the same apta-credit.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Transfers trust from the verified to the unverified — the same physician whose drsta prescriptions cure is trusted on unseen pathology; the same Veda verified in drsta domestic rites is trusted on heaven. Isvara as parama-apta seals the Vedic half: authorless sentences cannot intend, but an omniscient intender guarantees the unseen fruit. Denying adrstartha testimony while accepting drstartha is arbitrary surgery on one instrument.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Notes the division answers 1.1.7's latent question — how far the apta's sight extends — and previews 1.1.9's prameya-list, where soul-through-release are precisely the adrstartha objects testimony must carry. PANINI: dvividha = dvi + vidhac (twofold); drsta = drs + kta (seen); adrsta = na + drsta; artha here = object-domain (visaya), not mere meaning.",
