    "translation": "Soul (Atman), Body (Sharira), Senses (Indriya), Objects of sense (Artha), Intellect (Buddhi), Mind (Manas), Activity (Pravritti), Fault (Dosha), Transmigration (Pretyabhava), Fruit (Phala), Pain (Dukha), and Release (Apavarga) are the objects of right knowledge (Prameya).",
    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Twelve prameyas are enumerated, joined by the particle 'tu' (but/and) which pivots from the pramana-topic to the prameya-topic. The order is architectonic: atman the enjoyer (bhokta) first; sarira its abode; indriya its instruments; artha its field; buddhi its cognition; manas its inner mediator; then the soteriological chain pravrtti-dosa-pretyabhava-phala-duhkha-apavarga already mapped in 1.1.2. False knowledge of exactly these — taking body for soul, pleasure for the good — is bondage; true knowledge of exactly these is release.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): The objector demands Vaisesika categories — substance, quality, action, universal — asking why twelve only. Reply: inclusion by implication. Bodies and senses ARE substances, cognitions and pleasures ARE qualities; the twelve subsume the ontology while selecting what must be truly known for apavarga. Counting sand-grains never liberates; knowing soul-through-release does. 'Prameyam' in the singular denotes the collective as one soteriological field, not twelve disconnected topics.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): On 'tu' as topic-differentiator (pramana-samapti, prameya-arambha): without it the list would read as more instruments. On order: bhoktr (enjoyer), bhoga-sadhana (abode-instruments-field), bhoga (cognition-experience), then heya-hana-upaya (chain-to-release) — a medical sequence matching the caturvyuha. Nyaya prameyas thus never deny Vaisesika padarthas; they re-cut reality along the wound-and-cure line.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Why buddhi AND manas both listed, though cognition needs mind? Because buddhi is the effect-quality to be examined (eternal or not? whose?) while manas is the atomic instrument-limit to be proved (1.1.16); collapsing them would erase the 1.1.15 vs 1.1.16 division of labour. Likewise pretyabhava-phala-duhkha are not redundant with 1.1.2's chain: there they were causal links, here they are knowable objects with distinct definitions (1.1.19-21).\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Notes each of the twelve receives its defining sutra immediately (1.1.10-22) — enunciation here, definition next — exemplifying trividha-sastra-pravrtti (uddesa now, laksana next, pariksa in Books 2-4). PANINI: the twelve form an itaretara-dvandva (copulative compound, each member retaining sense); 'tu' is bhedaka (differentiator); 'prameyam' = pra-mi + yat (that which is to be truly known).",
