    "translation": "Desire, aversion, volition, pleasure, pain, and intelligence are the marks (indicators) of the Soul.",
    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): The invisible soul is inferred from six marks, each severally sufficient (pratyeka-paksa) — desire pulls toward, aversion pushes away, volition strives, pleasure-pain are felt, cognition illuminates. Memory is the clincher: 'that same I who saw yesterday remembers today' demands one continuant across sleeps and years. The corpse test refutes the body-self: the same frame minus desire proves desire's owner has departed. The senses cannot own them either — each fixed to one object (eye to colour only) and perishing with its organ, while desire ranges across objects and outlives any single organ.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Against the Buddhist series (santana) of ownerless flashes: recognition ('this is that same pot I saw') and moral responsibility (he who acted reaps) both require the rememberer to BE the experiencer. A new flash cannot inherit another's debt, nor recognize another's vision. Desire and the rest are qualities (guna), and qualities require a substance-substrate (dravya) — that substance, distinct from earth-through-mind (shown under 1.1.3's samplava: desire is perceived by inner contact alone, unlike earthy colour), is the soul. Momentariness would make even 'I' unstatable.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): On 'iti' closing the list (thus — these six, severally). 'Jnana' here covers both experience (anubhava) and memory (smrti), proving endurance doubly. Prayatna (volition/effort) is fenced from iccha (desire): desire may sleep while effort acts (the reluctant student rising), so effort is an independent mark — willing, not merely wanting.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Cognition is an adventitious, non-eternal quality (agantuka-guna) of soul — against Mimamsa-Vedanta self-luminosity, which would make these marks pointless (the ever-shining needs no marks). Because cognition comes and goes while the 'I' persists through deep sleep, soul is proved as substrate, not as light. Desire's intentionality (of-this, against-that) further proves a unified nedant across sense-manifolds.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Cross-tallies each mark with its 1.1.3 samplava proof — word teaches soul, inference marks it, yoga-perception contacts it — so 1.1.10 is where samplava's convergence first pays its debt. PANINI: linga = lig + ghan? 'lingyate anumiyate anena' (that by which inferred); iccha = is + cha (desiring), dvesa = dvis + ghan, prayatna = pra-yat + ... (striving-forth), sukha = su + kha (good aperture), jnana = jna + lyut.",
