    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Five outer instruments are enumerated, each from its predominant element — nose from earth, tongue from water, eye from fire, skin from air, ear from ether — proved by each manifesting its elemental quality (the nose grasps earthy smell because earthy itself). 'Bhutebhyah' (from elements) is stated to refute Samkhya derivation from ahankara (ego-principle): what is restricted to grasping colour, being damaged by excess light, and perishing with the eyeball cannot be a modification of conscious ego-stuff.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Three battles. (1) Against Samkhya: non-material ahankara cannot explain why each sense grasps exactly one elemental quality, nor why elemental excess (blinding glare, deafening roar) destroys function — material senses damaged by material excess proves material origin. (2) Against the single-sense theory (touch alone, all else its modification): sight grasps colour where no touch occurs (distant banner), and each sense has its fixed scope proved in 3.1 — reduction erases visaya-vyavastha. (3) Why five only (parisankhya): no sixth outer sense is ever evidenced; mind, the sixth cognizer, is inner and proved separately in 1.1.16. 'Bhutebhyah' in the plural signals mixture — each sense contains all five elements with one predominant, like a lamp-flame fed by oil, wick, and air yet called fiery.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): Distinguishes golaka (seat — the visible eyeball, nostril) from indriya proper (the subtle elemental power within, itself beyond sense-perception, inferred from function). 'Bhutebhyah' ablative denotes material cause (upadana-karana), not mere locus: the power is earthy-stuff configured for smelling. This saves the sutra from the charge that we see the eye yet never see 'the eye' — we see its seat.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Elemental damage is decisive — excess of the homogeneous (too much light) injures, excess of the heterogeneous cures (darkness rests the eye); an ego-product would answer to no elemental regimen. Udayana also ties bhautikatva to pratyaksa-jyaisthya: only material contact (sannikarsa) yields the quenching perception of 1.1.3's primacy doctrine.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Notes the nose-to-ear order follows gross-to-subtle elements (earth to ether), matching 1.1.13's sequence — pedagogy mirrors physics. PANINI: indriyam = indra (the soul as lord) + ghan? 'indrasya lingam/srstam' (belonging to/created for the lord); ghrana = ghra + ... (smelling), caksus = caks + ... (seeing power), srotra = sru + ... (hearing).",
