    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): The five bhutas are enumerated as the material substrate of bodies, senses, and sense-objects. Each is marked by its specific quality (visesa-guna) detailed in 1.1.14 — smell for earth through sound for ether — with cumulative inherence: earth bears smell, taste, colour, and touch; water bears all but smell; fire bears colour and touch; air bears touch alone among tangibles; ether bears sound alone. 'Iti' closes the list restrictively: exactly five.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Why enumerate elements among prameyas at all? Because the senses' origin (1.1.12) and the objects' nature (1.1.14) both depend on them — physics grounds epistemology. Against the four-element Buddhists who drop akasa: without ether, sound would inhere in nothing (a quality needs a substance-substrate), and the all-pervading medium proven by sound travelling everywhere would vanish. Against darkness-as-sixth-substance: darkness is light's absence (or earthy particles unlit), needing no new dravya; 'iti' (parisankhya) bars tamas and any sixth pretender.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): The nose-to-ether order runs gross-to-subtle, each subtler pervading the grosser's gaps — earth is most compounded, ether partless and vibhu (all-pervading). Cumulative inherence is not confusion: earth is still 'smell-possessing' specifically because smell inheres in earth alone among elements, though earth hosts others; specificity means unique locus, not sole content.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Ether as dravya is proved from sound-as-quality (gunatvat sabdasya): whatever bears a real quality is a substance, and sound is real (produced, grasped, subclassified into phonemes). Against Mimamsa sound-as-action (vibhu producers need no medium): action perishes with effort, but echoes and transmitted recitation persist and travel — persistence plus travel demands a pervasive substrate, akasa.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies bhuta-enumeration with 1.1.12's senses and 1.1.14's objects as one triptych (substrate–instrument–field), so the three sutras must be read as a single physics lesson. PANINI: bhutam = bhu + kta (that which has come into being); prthivi = prath + ... (the extensive); ap = ap + ... (waters, always plural); tejas = tij + ... (the shining); vayu = va + ... (blowing); akasam = a-kas + ... (open all round).",
