    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Pravrtti is arambha — undertaking, the volitional initiation of vocal, mental, and bodily operation. Threefold with moral valence each way: bodily giving versus theft, vocal truth versus lying, mental compassion versus envy. 'Iti' closes the list: exactly these three, no fourth. As in 1.1.2, the term metonymically carries its unseen residue — dharma and adharma (merit-demerit, adrsta) — as 'food is life' names the sustainer for the sustained; momentary acts endure as karmic configuration compelling rebirth.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Why 'arambha' (undertaking) rather than 'karman' (act)? Because initiation captures the will-element: trembling, digestion, and reflex drift are motions, not pravrtti. Against the Vaisesika reduction to mere movement (karman as upward-throwing etc.): Nyaya's pravrtti is purposive undertaking WITH moral residue — movement describes the body, undertaking describes the agent. Against the Buddhist momentariness puzzle (how can a perishing act cause a future birth?): the act perishes, its dharma-adharma configuration persists as unseen potency in the soul-series, ripening later — continuity of residue, not of act.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Buddhi' here is manas/antahkarana (the inner organ in operation), NOT 1.1.15's buddhi-cognition — else mind-action would collapse into cognition and the threefold would shrink to two. 'Arambha' fences out involuntary motions: only prayatna-purvaka (volition-preceded) commencements count, which is why deep-sleep breathing accrues no fresh karma.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): All three 1.1.10 marks converge in willed action — iccha aims, jnana guides, prayatna executes — so pravrtti is the soul's fullest outward self-manifestation. Deliberate restraint (nivrtti/uparama) is purposeful non-undertaking, not mere absence: the sage's stillness is as willed as the merchant's venture, which is why cessation itself can be an upaya (means) toward apavarga rather than a karmic vacuum.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies pravrtti's position as the third link of 1.1.2's chain — born of dosa, bearing janma — so its definition here teaches what exactly must be starved by defect-cessation. PANINI: pravrtti = pra-vrt + ktin (proceeding-forth); arambha = a-rabh + ghan (taking-hold, undertaking); vac = vac + ... (speech); buddhi here = inner organ in operation.",
