    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Dosas are raga (attachment-craving: lust, greed, thirst/trsna), dvesa (aversion: anger, envy, malice), and moha (delusion as mithyajnana — taking the not-self for self, the painful for pleasant, the impure for pure, the transient for eternal). 'Pravartana-laksana' — their very mark is setting-in-motion: a fault is known by its urging toward dharma-adharma acts, as fire is known by burning. Hierarchy: moha is root; raga-dvesa are its branches — no one craves or hates without first mis-taking.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): 'Laksana' here is jnapaka (indicator), not constitutive definition alone: faults both indicate bondage-state and cause further activity. Why list faults among prameyas? Because they are heya (to be abandoned) — prameya includes the avoidable, not only the attainable. Objector: faults are qualities of soul; liberation destroying them destroys soul-hood? Reply: adventitious (agantuka) qualities, removable like dust on a mirror — the eternal soul persists, cleaned. Destroying moha directly starves raga-dvesa (root-and-branches), which starves pravrtti: the exact reverse chain of 1.1.2.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): Moha is fenced as viparyaya (inversion), specifically the fourfold mis-taking — anitya in nitya, asuci in suci, duhkha in sukha, anatma in atma. Raga is 'this is mine/desirable' appropriation born of the pleasant-inversion; dvesa 'this harms/must perish' born of the painful-contact. Order note: though some lists lead with raga, moha is logically first (mula) — craving presupposes the mis-taking that makes its object seem worthy.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Dosa-ksaya mechanics — tattvajnana destroys moha directly (like light ending darkness), raga-dvesa indirectly (like branches dying when roots are cut). This grounds apara-apavarga: the living sage, defects burnt, acts without propelling residue — action without pravartana, the 'burnt seed' that sprouts no rebirth. Udayana thus ties 1.1.18 to 1.1.2's two-tier release defended in the Introductory Vartika.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies dosa with 1.1.2's 'dosa' link and 4.1's dosa-pariksa (raga-dvesa-moha taxonomy, mohasya pradhanyam) — definition here, anatomy in Book 4. PANINI: dosa = dus + ... ('dusyati anena' — that by which corrupted; or 'dusyati' — that which faults); pravartana = pra-vrt + lyut? setting-forth into motion; laksana = laks + ... (marking, indicating).",
