    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Pretya-bhava = punarutpatti — having departed (pretya, died), coming-into-being-again. Not mere birth but re-association: an enduring soul, already qualified by dharma-adharma residue, taking up a new body-sense-mind apparatus. Birth is the soul's conjunction with a fresh fruition-field; death its disjunction. The compound's order matters — departure first, then becoming — refuting both birth-without-death (eternalist overflow) and death-without-becoming (annihilationist terminus).\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Against the annihilationists (ucchedavadins, Carvaka-adjacent): if death ended the person, karmic responsibility would die with each body — the murdered creditor's debt unpayable, the donor's merit ownerless. Memory and recognition across the gap ('I was happy then') already prove survival within one life; transmigration extends the same continuant across lives. Against beginning-seekers (demand a first birth): beginninglessness (anadi), proved in Book 4's transmigration examination (cause-effect continuity without start), dissolves the demand — asking for rebirth's first instance is like asking for space's edge.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Punar' (again) is load-bearing — it distinguishes pretyabhava from first-creation theories (Isvara creating souls ex nihilo each birth, Samkhya purusa-body conjunction as fresh). Re-petition presupposes the petendum: soul plus residue returning. 'Bhava' as becoming (not static being) captures the acquiring of new birth-state, genus, lifespan, and experience-stock (jati-ayur-bhoga).\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Rebirth needs a carrier of residue between frames — neither body (burnt) nor senses (dissolved) nor moment-flashes (ownerless) qualify; only the enduring soul with adrsta-configuration bridges the gap. Udayana ties pretyabhava to 1.1.2's janma-link and 4.1's nimitta-naimittika continuity: transmigration just IS the unbroken cause-effect (merit-demerit to embodiment) chain viewed across deaths.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Notes pretyabhava defined minimally here precisely because Books 3-4 will anatomize its mechanism (soul-debates, karma-transmission, Isvara-supervision) — uddesa brevity enabling pariksa depth. PANINI: pretya = pra-i + ... (having gone forth, died) + bhava = bhu + ... (becoming); punarutpatti = punar + ut + pad + ... (arising again).",
