    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Samsaya is visesa-apeksa vimarsa — wavering pondering that awaits the decisive mark. Four triggers: (1) samana-dharma — tallness common to man and post glimpsed at dusk ('man or post?'); (2) aneka-dharma — manifold/peculiar property, e.g., a heard sound whose source-kind is unsettled among many possibles; (3) vipratipatti — conflicting testimony of authorities (one sage: soul exists; another: it does not); (4) upalabdhi-anupalabdhi-avyavastha — irregularity, water found where expected then not. Doubt is neither ignorance (which lacks alternatives) nor error (which has settled wrongly): it holds both alternatives present, desiring the visesa that decides.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Pre-answers Book 2's no-doubt objector. 'Common property cannot cause doubt, since the specific is absent from it' — reply: doubt arises precisely BECAUSE the specific is awaited; absence of visesa with presence of common marks is the causal complex, not the common mark alone. Compound resolved: samanam anekasya dharmah (property common to many) for trigger one; aneka as manifold-peculiar for trigger two. The objector's 'true apprehension leaves no doubt' confuses decisive perception with the pre-decisive state defined here.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Visesa-apeksa' fences doubt from decided cognition (adhyavasaya): where the mark is grasped, wavering ends by definition. Vimarsa is distinct from jnana-claims — doubt asserts neither alternative, only their oscillation. Vipratipatti means authority-conflict (apta-vs-apta), not lay disagreement; avyavastha means pattern-breach (expected perception failing), not bare non-perception — else every unseen would doubtworthy, absurdly.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Doubt is jijnasa-janaka (inquiry-generator) — neither prama (valid) nor aprama (invalid) but the vimarsa that summons pramanas into play. Doubt has positive content: both alternatives are presented (ullikhita), not blank nescience. Udayana grades doubt — perceptual (post-or-man), testimonial (sage-conflict), inductive (water-irregularity) — each calling its proper resolver (closer look, sastra-weight, repeated trial).\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies samsaya with 1.1.1's list (doubt as second category because inquiry's gate) and 2.1's pariksa (where each trigger is attacked and saved) — definition here, trial there. PANINI: samsaya = sam-si + ... (lying-together of alternatives); vimarsa = vi-mrs + ... (pondering over); vipratipatti = vi-prati-pad + ... (going-against); upalabdhi = upa-labh + ...; avyavastha = a-vi-ava-stha (non steady abiding).",
