    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Prayojana is yam artham adhikrtya pravartate — the thing made one's basis with a view to which one acts. Two faces: ista-prapti (gaining the desirable) and anista-parihara (avoiding the undesirable). No agent stirs without it — not the farmer, not the debater, not the sastra itself, whose prayojana is the highest good through true knowledge. In debate, prayojana keeps discourse from drifting into purposeless chatter (nis-prayojana-katha): the parties must know whether they seek nirnaya or victory.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): The regress objector — if every act needs a prayojana, seeking prayojana needs one, ad infinitum. Reply: the series terminates in parama-prayojana (the highest purpose: cessation of pain), desired for itself, not for a further end — as the stair-climber's steps end in the terrace. Prayojana also classifies katha: vada's prayojana is tattva-nirnaya (truth-settling), jalpa's is vijaya via truth-defence, vitanda's mere pratipaksa-bhanga (opponent-breaking). Same outer moves, different inner aims — motive individuates debate-forms.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Adhikrtya' (having made the basis) proves prayojana is objective-correlate, not bare wish — the Havana-offering aims at heaven as attainable end, not fantasy. 'Artha' spans desirable and avoidable; prayojana is thus wider than phala (fruit actually reaped): the farmer's prayojana (harvest) may fail while acts persist, yet acts without any prayojana never begin. Sastra-prayojana (prompting the qualified student) differs from sastra-phala (what study yields) — confusing them breeds the 'purposeless treatise' charge.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Prayojana as pravartaka (motivator) completes the 1.1.10 triad outward — iccha aims AT prayojana, jnana maps means TO it, prayatna moves. Even liberation-seeking has prayojana (duhkha-nivrtti), else mumuksus would never study; the charge that the liberated act aimlessly is answered by loka-sangraha (world-uplift as post-release motive, cf. apara-apavarga teaching). Motivelessness belongs only to the released-in-absorption, never to the teacher.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies prayojana with 1.1.1's list (motive as fourth category because sastra must justify its own pursuit) and 1.2's katha-rules (each debate-form's legitimacy judged by its declared prayojana) — motive examined at both ends of Adhyaya 1. PANINI: prayojanam = pra-yuj + ... ('prayujyate anena/asya arthe' — that by/for which one yokes oneself to action); adhikrtya = adhi-kr + ... (having made the basis).",
