    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Drstanta requires buddhi-samya — the laukika (ordinary observer) and the pariksaka (trained examiner) cognize alike. Kitchen with fire-smoke, lake with neither: cook and logician agree. Such agreement proves the locus stable enough to carry vyapti — only what is jointly seen can be universalized. Without an admitted instance, inference floats (smoke-fire link never anchored) and testimony babbles (words about nowhere). The Introductory Bhasya already warned: only what is perceived in a known substratum can be inferred elsewhere or spoken of to others.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Why separately enunciate the obvious? Two reasons. (1) Dependence: inference and testimony both feed on examples — inference projects the seen-together, testimony reports the known; denying examples starves both. (2) The atheist dilemma (nastika-vyaghata): the denier of stable loci must either ADMIT an example — conceding something abides enough to illustrate, refuting flux/void — or DENY all examples — leaving his own denial unillustrated and unconvincing, since no reasoning convinces without 'as... so...'. Either way self-contradiction; hence drstanta deserves its own category as debate's fulcrum.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Samya' is sameness of cognition-content (same object-form grasped), not of words or expertise — the cook's 'hot bright kitchen' and the logician's 'fire-smoke locus' converge intentionally. Laukika-pariksaka pairing excludes two defectives: the fool (who doubts kitchen fire — unteachable) and the partisan (who denies for victory — vitandika). Only honest agreement counts, which is why drstanta grounds vada but merely decorates jalpa.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Drstanta as vyapti-grahaka-sthana (the place where concomitance is grasped): positive (kitchen) grasps anvaya, negative (lake) grasps vyatireka; induction completes only with both, as 1.1.36-37 will formalize. Udayana answers the Buddhist particularist (each instance unique, no universal graspable): if kitchen-fire shared nothing with hill-fire, the words 'fire' and 'smoke' themselves could never recur meaningfully — language-use presupposes the very sameness drstanta exhibits.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies drstanta with udaharana (1.1.36-37, the example INSIDE proof) — drstanta is the admitted stock, udaharana its deployed citation; stock must precede deployment, hence category 5 precedes members 7. PANINI: drstanta = drsta + anta (end/limit of what is seen — the settled instance); laukika = loka + ... (of the world, ordinary); pariksaka = pari-iks + ... (all-round examiner).",
