    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Siddhanta is samsthiti (firm standing) reached through tantra (an established school-corpus), adhikarana (a topic-context whose proof carries further), or abhyupagama (an assumed concession). It is not bare opinion (mata) nor thesis-under-proof (paksa), but what survives examination and can bear weight as premise. The compound's three disjuncts cover the three ways tenets get authority: tradition, entailment, grant.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Parses the dvandva — tantra ca adhikaranam ca abhyupagamas ca, with samsthiti as the common result (standing-firm THROUGH each). Why exactly three means? Authority must come from somewhere shared (school), something proved (topic), or something conceded (grant) — no fourth source exists: private fancy convinces none, bare assertion proves nothing. The definition thus fences siddhanta from both dogmatism (unexamined fancy) and skepticism (nothing stands).\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): Tantra = the school's settled corpus (siddhanta-samuha, e.g., Vaisesika padarthas for its followers); adhikarana = prakarana, the presented topic whose establishment drags its ground along; abhyupagama = the not-yet-examined (apariksita) starting grant; samsthiti = avasana, the settled conclusion (nirnaya-stage of that item). Each maps to one of the four kinds next (1.1.27-31): tantra births the first two, adhikarana the third, abhyupagama the fourth.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Siddhanta vs paksa — the paksa is what THIS debate must settle, siddhanta what stands settled behind it; confusing them begs (proving paksa by paksa) or stalls (re-proving siddhanta endlessly). All debate presupposes siddhantas the way walking presupposes ground — the skeptic demanding proof of everything at once demands walking without ground.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Notes siddhanta defined here as category 6 precisely because 1.2's vada-rule ('not opposed to established tenets') needs it — definition placed exactly where debate-theory will spend it. PANINI: siddhanta = sidh + ... (established end/conclusion); tantram = tan + ... (loom, extended doctrine); adhikaranam = adhi-kr + ... (substratum, topic-context); abhyupagama = abhi-upa-gam + ... (coming-near, accepting); samsthiti = sam-stha + ... (standing firm together).",
