    "translation": "The dogma of all schools, a dogma peculiar to some school, a hypothetical dogma, and an implied dogma—these are the four kinds of established tenets.",
    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): The four — sarva-tantra (of all schools), prati-tantra (of one school against another), adhikarana (topic-entailing), abhyupagama (assumed) — rest on arthantara-bhava (difference of object/purpose): each exists for a different debate-need. Universal tenets supply common ground, school-tenets define party-lines, topic-tenets carry consequences, assumed tenets start inquiry. 'Arthantara' also signals the examples differ in what they establish, not merely in words.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Why four, neither three nor five? Exhaustion proof: a tenet is authoritative either by whose-it-is (all schools or some school) or by how-it-functions (entailing or granted) — source-division gives two, function-division gives two, and source and function cross-cut without remainder. A fifth (e.g., 'self-evident') collapses into sarva-tantra (if all admit) or abhyupagama (if merely granted). The four are thus jointly exhaustive and mutually exclusive in purpose, though one sentence may serve two roles across debates.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): Order is descending scope — universal, then particular, then implicit-by-topic, then merely granted — mirroring how debate narrows from shared ground to live dispute to tactical concession. 'Bhavat' (being) marks ontological standing, not verbal mention: each kind genuinely STANDS (samsthiti) in its sphere, or it would not bind its parties.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Burden-of-proof mapping — sarva-tantra needs no proof within tradition (denial is self-defeat), prati-tantra needs proof against opponents, adhikarana needs proof of the topic (consequence free), abhyupagama needs no proof until its details are examined (then either confirmed or abandoned). Umpires use this table to assign who proves what: most nigrahasthana disputes are burden-disputes in disguise.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies the four with 1.1.26's three means — tantra births the first two (universal/particular by scope), adhikarana the third, abhyupagama the fourth — closing the loop opened one sutra earlier. PANINI: caturvidha = catur + vidha + ... (fourfold); sarva = sr + ... (all, whole); prati = ... (toward, against, particular); arthantara = artha + antara (other object/purpose); bhava = bhu + ... (being).",
