    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Abhyupagama-siddhanta is apariksita-abhyupagamat tad-visesa-pariksanam — from an unexamined grant, examination of its particulars. Stock cases: debating the soul's qualities grants that soul exists; debating cause-kinds grants that causation holds; worshipping with Vedic rites grants their efficacy. The grant is unexamined (apariksita) not unexaminable — inquiry must start somewhere, and starting points are held hostage to their own detail-work: survive it, they stand confirmed; fail it, they fall with their details.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Estoppel logic — one who argues attributes of X cannot later deny X without defeating himself; the opponent may hold him to the implication. This differs from adhikarana (1.1.30): there proof precedes consequence, here grant precedes examination. Confusing them lets debaters claim proved status for mere grants (inflation) or demand full proof before any inquiry begins (paralysis). The sutra licenses midpoints: begin granted, end proved-or-abandoned.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Apariksita' fenced as not-yet-examined (not beyond-examination like apta-mysteries): abhyupagama belongs to inquiry's doorway, not its sanctum. 'Tad-visesa' (its particulars) means the grant's own differentiations — soul's qualities, cause's kinds, rite's steps — examined one by one; 'pariksanam' the testing that either promotes the grant to adhikarana-grade (now proved, entailing) or demotes it to rejection. Every science lives on such working hypotheses.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Debate-economy formalized — proving everything before beginning proves nothing ever (infinite preface); granting working bases lets inquiry bankrupt or solvent-test them. Udayana's own Isvara-proof begins abhyupagama-grade (grant seekers and a world-order needing explanation), promotes through karya-hetu examination to adhikarana-grade (proved God entailing providence) — the Kusumanjali as lived movement across 1.1.31 to 1.1.30.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies abhyupagama with 1.1.27's arthantara (its distinct purpose is inquiry-starting) and 2.1's opening objections (each purvapaksa begins as the opponent's abhyupagama granted arguendo, then examined to destruction) — the license under which Books 2-4 stage hostile views. PANINI: abhyupagama = abhi-upa-gam + ... (coming-near to accept); apariksita = a-pari-iks + ... (unexamined); visesa = vi-sis + ... (distinguishing particular); pariksana = pari-iks + ... (all-round testing).",
