    "translation": "A Proposition (Pratijna) is the declaration of what is to be established.",
    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Pratijna is sadhya-nirdesa — pointing-out (nirdesa) of the sadhya (what is to be established by this reasoning): 'the hill is fiery'. It must be doubtful yet provable: neither siddha (already admitted — no need) nor asadhya (impossible — no competence, like 'the sky-lotus is fragrant'). Nirdesa excludes mere utterance (a parrot's 'hill fiery' is no pratijna) — declaration WITH undertaking-to-prove.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Pratijna fixes the burden (bhara-vyavastha): without it hetu wanders (proving what?), example floats, and viruddha cannot be judged (contradiction against WHAT?). Pratijna-antara (shifting thesis mid-debate: 'hill fiery... I meant luminous') is already implicitly barred — the declared sadhya binds. The same sentence returns as nigamana (1.1.39): first as doubtful-to-prove, last as proved — identical words, transformed status, which is why restatement is no repetition-fault.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Sadhya' is sadhayitavya — to-be-established-BY-THIS-reasoning (not absolutely unknown: the hill is seen, its fieriness doubted). 'Nirdesa' is specification distinguishing paksa (subject) from sadhya-property: 'hill' named as locus, 'fiery' as probandum — conflating them (saying only 'fire!') states no proposition. Pratijna-virodha (contradicting one's own tenet, 5.2) is previewed: the sadhya must cohere with the proponent's siddhanta, else defeat at birth.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Paksa-hood conditions (paksata): the subject must lack decisive cognition either way (sandigdha) AND be desired-to-infer (bubhutsita) — a settled perceiver needs no pratijna, a disinterested hearer receives none. Udayana's sighatara test: pratijna must be singular and affirmative-capable ('hill fiery', not 'hill either fiery or not' — disjunction proves nothing, inviting prakaranasama).\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies pratijna with sabda-membership (proposition embodies testimony, 1.1.1's synthesis) and with 1.2's vada-rule (paksa-pratipaksa-parigraha presupposes stated theses on both sides). PANINI: pratijna = prati-jna + ... (declaration toward/against — counter-knowing made public); nirdesa = nir-dis + ... (pointing-out, specification); sadhya = sadh + ... (to be accomplished).",
