    "translation": "Also, from its dissimilarity to the example.",
    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): 'Tatha' (similarly, with equal validity) extends hetu to vaidharmya-formulation — through dissimilarity to the negative example: 'because it is not unlike the fiery... ' i.e., 'because, unlike the lake which lacking fire lacks smoke, this hill possesses smoke-linked fire'. The hetu-property is stated via double negation or contrapositive, carrying identical probative force where the contrapositive concomitance (fire-absence to smoke-absence) holds.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Vyatireki-hetu legitimized — contrapositive vyapti (yatra vahnyabhavas tatra dhumabhavas, where no fire there no smoke, lake-displayed) grounds negative reasoning as firmly as anvaya grounds positive. This founds kevala-vyatireki inference (negative-only: 'earth differs from other substances because it has smell' — no positive sapaksa exists exhibiting smell-plus-difference, only the negative exclusion operates). Against those (some Buddhists, some Naiyayikas' opponents) allowing only anvaya: they'd forfeit all difference-proofs (soul distinct from body, ether distinct from earth), which proceed precisely by exclusion — amputating half of reason.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Vaidharmya' is vipaksa-dissimilarity (unlikeness to the property-absent locus), not bare difference (hill differs from lake in size too — irrelevant). 'Tatha' marks parity of validity, not identity of procedure: negative hetu needs vyatireka-vyapti grasped in the negative example, mirroring anvaya-grasp in the positive. Together 1.1.34-35 license the standard double illustration (kitchen positively, lake negatively) that seals concomitance against accidental co-presence.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Kevala-vyatireki defended as genuine inference (not disguised perception or testimony): the vyatireka-vyapti is itself grasped (by repeated non-observation of smell apart from earth-difference), then applied — same cognitive machinery, negative sign. Udayana's test: negative-only reasoning is valid iff no sapaksa is even possible in principle (difference, earth-ness); where sapaksa exists but is ignored, the reasoner is merely lazy, not kevala-vyatireki.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies 1.1.35 with 1.1.37 (negative example) as hetu-side and example-side of one vyatireka coin — hetu may be stated negatively because examples may be displayed negatively; symmetry of the five members preserved. PANINI: tatha = ta + ... (thus, similarly); vaidharmya = vi-dharma + ... (dissimilarity, unlikeness in property).",
