    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Five hetvabhasas — semblances (abhasa) of hetu, like crystal appearing red near a rose: looking probative, failing one hetu-condition each. The erratic strays from pervasion, the contradictory pervades the opposite, the question-equal leaves doubt unmoved, the unproved lacks subject-presence, the mistimed arrives irrelevant. Each defined severally next (1.2.5-9); the list is restrictive (iti implied): exactly these five genera, subdivisions within.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): The puzzle — if discussion (1.2.1-2) proceeds by pramana-tarka, how can fallacies be URGED in it at all? Reply through semblance: the proponent mistakes the fallacy for valid (abhasa fools the USER in good-faith error, sometimes in bad-faith hope), putting it forward AS valid; the opponent then exposes it. So fallacies enter via delusion, are judged by knowledge — enumeration here trains the judge, not the cheat. Restriction puzzle (why do only SOME clinchers appear in a given bout, though all are listed?): things follow peculiar character (svabhava), not debaters' commands — commanding fallacies to appear is ridicule-worthy; describing their incidence is science. Hence hetvabhasa-theory belongs to debate while vitiating no honest vada: vada USES hetu, jalpa ABUSES hetvabhasa, both need the grid.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Abhasa' is sadrsa-abhasa (semblance through similarity to hetu): each fallacy mimics one hetu-face — savyabhicara mimics pervasion (present, but also elsewhere), viruddha mimics ground (present, but proving opposite), prakaranasama mimics relevance (topical, but undeciding), sadhyasama mimics presence (stated, but unproved), kalatita mimics timeliness (stated, but stale). Diagnosis is thus differential: name which face is mimicked, and the cure follows (show deviation, inversion, circularity, unprovedness, staleness).\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Five genera cover all hetu-condition failures exhaustively — vyapti-failure (savyabhicara), vipaksa-pervasion (viruddha), indecision (prakaranasama), paksadharmata-failure (sadhyasama), occasion-failure (kalatita). Later logic's sixfold (adding badhita/svarupa-asiddha splits) subdivides kalatita and sadhyasama without adding genera: Gautama's five are determinables, later forms determinates. Udayana's sighatara: hetvabhasa is relative to the PROPOSED hetu — same smoke valid for fire, fallacious for 'hill is golden' (deviation) — fallacyhood is relational, never intrinsic to words.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies the five with 1.1.33-39's members (each fallacy corrupts one member: viruddha the hetu, siddha-sadhana the udaharana's admittance, kalatita the upanaya's timing) and with Book 5's fallacy-pariksa (definition here, anatomy there). PANINI: hetvabhasa = hetu + abhasa (a-bhas + ... shining-forth as if, semblance); the five a dvandva of genera; savyabhicara = sa-vi-abhi-car + ... (going along with deviation); viruddha = vi-rudh + ... (obstructed-opposite); prakaranasama = prakarana + sama (equal to the question); sadhyasama = sadhya + sama; kalatita = kala + atita (time-transcended).",
