    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Savyabhicara is anaikantika — not-restricted-to-one-end (eka-anta): 'sound is eternal because intangible' strays, since intangibility occurs in eternal souls AND non-eternal intellects — proving neither end (naikantika). Vyabhicara (deviation) is the hetu's wandering beyond the sadhya: present where sadhya is absent. The stock test: show the hetu in a vipaksa (soul-less? no — sadhya-absent locus like intellect for eternality) and errance is proved.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Threefold subdivision — (1) sadharana (ordinary-deviant: intangible, found both sides); (2) asadharana (peculiar: 'sound is eternal because sound-ness' — occurring ONLY on the subject, never as sapaksa/vipaksa, so pervasion ungraspable); (3) anupasamhari (uncombinable: 'all is momentary because existent' — no locus outside 'all' to display sapaksa, vipaksa impossible). All three share anaikantikatva: inconclusive between ends. Against the charge that asadharana is really asiddha (unproved): no — sound-ness IS present (not unproved), only pervasion-less (inconclusive) — presence without pervasion is errance, not absence.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Eka-anta' (one end) means sadhya-paksa decisively: the valid hetu terminates (anta) at exactly one end (sadhya-presence, vipaksa-absence); the erratic terminates at neither, wandering both. Vacaspati grades deviation — vyabhicara-darsana (seen deviation, fatal), vyabhicara-sanka (suspected deviation, prompting tarka-check), vyabhicara-asambhava (deviation impossible, valid) — turning diagnosis into procedure: suspect, test by vipaksa-search, conclude.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Inconclusiveness formalized — hetu is anaikantika iff sapaksa-sattva and vipaksa-sattva BOTH hold (or pervasion ungraspable as in peculiar/uncombinable). Udayana's sighatara: doubt about deviation must be PRAMANA-backed (a cited vipaksa-locus), not fancied — else every hetu falls to imagined counterexamples (anavastha of suspicion). The anupasamhari case grounds kevala-anvayi protection: 'knowable because nameable' has no vipaksa possible, yet pervasion holds positively — uncombinable is NOT automatically fallacious unless both ends genuinely compete.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies savyabhicara with hetu-condition one (vyapti) failing while paksadharmata holds — the commonest debate-fault, hence listed first; and with Book 5's sixteen hetvabhasa-varieties (ordinary/peculiar/uncombinable anatomized with sixteen probans-kinds). PANINI: savyabhicara = sa-vi-abhi-car + ... (going along with deviation); anaikantika = an-eka-anta + ... (not one-ended); vyabhicara = vi-abhi-car + ... (going astray).",
