    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Na tat arthantara-bhavat — no, because of DIFFERENCE in alternative-status (artha-antara-bhava): vak-chhala's alternative is homonym-switch (nava-new to nava-nine, both PRIMARY senses), upacara-chhala's is primary-secondary confusion (scaffold-wood primary to scaffold-dwellers secondary). The alternatives differ IN KIND (slesa-kind vs laksana-kind), so artha-antara-bhava (differing of alternatives) sustains the species — same genus (imposition), different differentiae (what is imposed from what).\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Diagnostic test — vak-chhala is cured by DISAMBIGUATION (specify nutana, the nine-reading dies with no remainder), upacara-chhala only by TRANSFER-RECALL (recall dwellers-purpose, literalism dies): different cures prove different diseases (a fever-cure failing a fracture proves distinct ailments). Were both mere 'alternative-meaning', one cure would serve both — it does not. The p.609 parallel vindicated: just as jati-hood survives example-variation (many jatis, one genus), chhala-hood survives alternative-variation (three species, one genus) — variation in the ILLUSTRATIVE does not touch the CONSTITUTIVE.\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Arthantara-bhavat' parsed as arthayoh antara-bhavat (difference OF the two alternatives): vak's pair (new/nine) are co-primary coordinates, upacara's pair (wood/dwellers) are primary-secondary hierarchy — coordinate-switch vs hierarchy-flattening. Ablative (bhavat) marks GROUND: it is BY this difference that non-identity holds. Vacaspati notes the reply concedes the objector's genus (both impose alternatives) while denying exhaustion — respectful refutation, not dismissal.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Remainder-formalized — vak remainder: slesa-dependence (remove homonymy, vak-chhala impossible: unambiguous 'cow' admits no vak-twist); upacara remainder: transfer-dependence (remove figurative institution, upacara-chhala impossible: literal-only language admits no literalizing). Each species has a REMOVAL-CONDITION the other lacks — the strongest difference-proof: X exists iff condition Cx, Y iff Cy, Cx ≠ Cy, therefore X ≠ Y. Collapse would require shared removal-conditions; none exist.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies 1.2.16 with 1.2.12/1.2.14's mechanisms (slesa vs transfer, exhibited there, weaponized here) — the reply merely points back at the mechanisms already displayed: division defended by its own exhibits. PANINI: na = ... (no, negation); tat = ... (that, the collapse); arthantara-bhavat = artha + antara + ... (ablative: from/because of difference of alternatives).",
