    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Avisese va kincit-sadharmyat eka-chhala-prasangah — if in non-difference, then from SOME similarity, single-quibble contingency follows: deny ALL difference between vak and upacara and even their slight similarity (both verbal tricks) forces merger into ONE chhala. The objector's avisesa devours his own dvaita (two): pressing non-difference consistently, why stop at two? Some-similarity links all three (word, genus, figure are all verbal) — collapse to two collapses to one, and the objector who came to halve ends with nothing to distinguish even his objection from its target.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Prasanga (contingency-pressure) generalized — non-difference is infectious: grant avisese for vak-upacara, and samanya-chhala (also alternative-imposing, also verbal) catches it by the same kincit-sadharmya (some-similarity: all use words). The objector cannot quarantine his collapse: similarity-chains ignore quarantine. Hence 1.2.16's difference must hold, or debate-theory loses chhala-plurality entirely — and with it the umpire's diagnostic vocabulary (naming WHICH trick was used becomes impossible if all tricks are one).\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Va' (or/if) marks prasanga-conditional: IF avisesa, THEN eka-prasanga — the consequent's absurdity (no diagnostic vocabulary, yet umpires daily distinguish homonym-twists from literalizings) refutes the antecedent by modus tollens. 'Kincit-sadharmyat' (from some similarity): SOME similarity always exists between any two things (both expressible, both debated) — were some-similarity enough for identity, all categories would merge into one (pramana = prameya, both knowables!) — reductio of similarity-sufficient-identity, protecting ALL divisions, not just chhala's.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Similarity-identity confusion diagnosed as the objector's disease — sadharmya (sharing A property) vs tadatmya (being THE SAME thing): twins share faces, remain two persons. Udayana's sighatara: demand the objector exhibit IDENTITY-conditions (same removal-conditions, same cures, same mechanisms), not similarity-sightings — sightings are cheap (everything resembles everything somehow), conditions are dear. The objector shows sightings; Gautama demands conditions — mismatch decides for plurality.\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies 1.2.17 closing the triptych (enumerate-object-defend-defend: double defence, objection bracketed both sides) with Adhyaya 1's approaching colophon (1.2.20: multiplicity from vikalpa — differences multiply legitimately) — plurality vindicated as method, single-quibble contingency dismissed as methodlessness. PANINI: avisesa = a-vi-sesa + ... (non-difference); kincit = kim + ... (some, a little); sadharmyat = sa-dharma + ... (ablative: from similarity); prasanga = pra-sanj + ... (contingent consequence, forced occasion).",
