    "translation": "A Point of Defeat (Nigrahasthana) arises when one misunderstands, or does not understand at all.",
    "commentary": "BHASYA (Vatsyayana): Nigrahasthana is vipratipatti (perverse understanding — misconstruing proposition, hetu, or example) and apratipatti (non-understanding — mute bewilderment when reply is due). Both are genus: every specific defeat (contradicting tenet, abandoning thesis, irrelevant speech, unintelligible jargon, admitting opponent's view...) is EITHER misunderstanding the debate-state OR failing to grasp it. The umpire (madhyastha) declares defeat not as punishment but recognition: nirnaya can no longer result from this bout, so it ends.\n\nVARTIKA (Uddyotakara): Against restricting nigrahasthana to the shameless (only cheats deserve defeat): misunderstanding is itself censurable in one who VOLUNTEERED as knower — volunteering undertaken, competence owed. The assembly convened on that credit; squandering it wrongs the conveners. Against expanding to every stammer (nervousness = defeat?): vipratipatti means STRUCTURAL misgrasp (taking hetu as proposition, example as thesis), apratipatti PERSISTENT blankness after due time — momentary hesitation earns prompting, not defeat. Umpire discretion is thus fenced both ways: neither laxity (endless second chances) nor cruelty (one stammer, death).\n\nTATPARYA (Vacaspati): 'Vipratipatti' (vi-prati-pad: going-against in understanding) covers active errors — pratijna-virodha (self-contradiction), hetvabhasa-use exposed, siddhanta-virodha, artha-antara (answering another question), nirarthaka (meaningless speech); 'apratipatti' (a-prati-pad: non-arrival at understanding) covers passive failures — ajnana (confessed ignorance), avijnana (incomprehension of opponent's point), viksepa (evasion), matanujna (conceding opponent's view). Genus-species preview: Book 5's 22 are these two writ large — active/passive defeat-families.\n\nPARISUDDHI (Udayana): Defeat-conditions formalized — nigraha occurs iff (i) a duty of the bout is breached (prove, answer, adhere) AND (ii) breach is attributable (not thunder, illness, assembly-riot — force majeure excuses). Udayana grades: incurable (self-contradiction — no repair possible), curable-once (misspeaking correctable on prompt), procedural (time-expiry — bout ends, honour intact). Only the first two stain; the third merely closes — defeat (parajaya) distinguished from adjournment (visrama).\n\nBHASHYACHANDRA (Raghuttama): Tallies nigrahasthana-genus with 1.2.20's bahutva (multiplicity announced here, 22 anatomized in Book 5) and with 1.2.1-3's debate-rules (each rule's breach deposits one defeat-species: vada-breach yields pratijna-virodha, jalpa-breach yields chhala-persistence, vitanda-breach yields sthapana-smuggling) — definition, rules, and anatomy triangulated. PANINI: nigrahasthana = ni-grah + ... (holding-down place, point of defeat); vipratipatti = vi-prati-pad + ... (going-against in understanding); apratipatti = a-prati-pad + ... (non-arrival at understanding).",
